findings.body:
The class-level `@MainActor` is removed from `EditBookmarkViewModelTests`, but the tests call `EditBookmarkViewModel.selectFolder(_:)`, which is annotated `@MainActor` in `EditBookmarkViewModel.swift`. Those test methods are synchronous and non-isolated, so calling `selectFolder` from them is an isolation violation. Depending on the language mode this is a compile error under Swift 6 or a new warning under Swift 5, which is the opposite of what this PR is meant to do.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/ClientTests/Library/Bookmarks/EditBookmarkViewModelTests.swift" lines="8-14"}

The `setUp`/`tearDown` conversion here is fine. The problem is only the dropped annotation. Restoring `@MainActor` on the class is the consistent fix, matching the other suites in this PR.

In `testRequestAuthorization`, the `XCTAssertTrue` calls were replaced with Swift's `assert(_:_:)`. This is a regression. `assert` does not record an XCTest failure. It traps the test host, so a failure looks like a crash rather than a failed assertion. It is also compiled out of optimized builds, so the check can silently disappear. The mock calls its completion synchronously, so the check does run in this suite, but the wrong tool is being used.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/ClientTests/Helpers/NotificationManagerTests.swift" lines="25-30"}

Keep `XCTAssertTrue` here. The mock invokes the completion synchronously, so no expectation is needed. The `[center]` capture and `center?.requestAuthorizationWasCalled ?? false` are awkward; `testGetNotificationSettings` in the same file uses `XCTAssertTrue` directly.

`MockUserNotificationCenter` is now marked `@unchecked Sendable`, but it has several mutable `var`s such as `pendingRequests`, `requestAuthorizationWasCalled` and `getSettingsWasCalled`. `@unchecked` tells the compiler to skip the check. That is a reasonable way to silence the warning for a test double used synchronously, but the compiler will no longer flag misuse from a background context.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/ClientTests/Mocks/MockUserNotificationCenter.swift" lines="9-9"}

If the mock is only used on the main actor, `@MainActor` on the class is a stricter and more honest annotation. If it has to be used off the main actor, a one-line comment explaining why `@unchecked` is safe would help the next reader.
